items.text:
Discuss a local traditional territory where Michif French was/is spoken, with guiding questions such as:
• How did this community come to be?;
• Who was originally there?;
• Who were/are the settlers?;
• Who shared the land?;
• How were Michif/Métis people displaced (e.g., road allowance people, Scrip)?
• What relationships were built amongst local First Nations?;
• How were relationships/decisions formed? (e.g., kinship ties within Treaty territory); and,
• How has it transformed over the years?

Discuss Métis core values and how they play a role in personal identity and interactions with others, such as:
• iltay forr (strength);
• bonn cheur or keur (kindness);
• kooraazh (courage);
• anndjurii (tolerance);
• honnet (honesty);
• rispay (respect);
• l’aamoor (love);
• patarzhii (sharing);
• praanswaen (caring);
• balaansii (balance);
• pashiaans (patience);
• eumeur (humour); and;
• conexion avek la Mayr de Tayr (connection to Mother Earth).

Discuss how la manyair dis prominii can be a way of learning from one another by considering questions such as:
• What protocols should be followed when hosting?;
• How did community members visit in the past?;
• How do community members visit currently?;
• What do we do to prepare for visitors?;
• How do we arrive as visitors?; and,
• Why is it vital that we continue to learn from one another through visiting?

Explore traditional Michif/Métis ways of life such as:
• harvesting medicines;
• using natural medicines for holistic health;
• gardening;
• preserving foods;
• cooking/baking;
• storytelling;
• connecting to the buffalo;
• farming; and,
• practising equine husbandry.

Participate in hosting local traditional and contemporary Michif/Métis gatherings such as:
• cultural ceremonies;
• school events;
• community celebrations (e.g., weddings, anniversaries, wakes, funerals, holidays, kitchen parties, Back to Batoche festival); and,
• visiting opportunities.

Explore opportunities to participate in the creation of traditional and contemporary Michif/Métis fashion and visual arts such as:
• ribbon skirts, moccasins, Métis sash;
• hide and leather clothing, Hudson Bay Company capoo, accessories;
• fire bags, octopus bags;
• beadwork, embroidery, sewing, quilting;
• braided rugs, tufting;
• birchbark art; and,
• painting, photography.

Explore opportunities to participate in traditional and contemporary Michif/Métis music such as:
• fiddle music;
• jigging, square dancing;
• Métis music; and,
• songs (e.g., Pierre Falcon’s 1816 Battle of Seven Oaks Song/Chanson de la Grenouillère, community-built songs that reference the voyageurs, call-and-response songs, lullabies).

Prepare to interpret written texts and oral communications, before reading, listening and viewing, using strategies such as:
• making predictions;
• using prior knowledge;
• using personal experience; and,
• visual aids.

Interpret written texts and oral communications, during reading and listening, using strategies such as:
• identifying supplementary information to help construct meaning of written texts and oral communications (e.g., summary, illustrations, translations);
• recognizing physical (e.g., gestures and facial expressions) and auditory (e.g., laughter, volume, tone) cues;
• listing context clues and key words to make connections between new information, prior knowledge and personal experience;
• identifying different spelling and pronunciation from another language (e.g., table - la table [French] - lii taab [Michif French]) to decipher words; and,
• discussing strategies to help cope with information containing unknown elements (e.g., ask for clarification or repetition when needed, indicate lack of understanding).

Describe the use of present tense verbs and commands (lii moo dakchion), including:
• asi tway (sit down);
• lev tway (get up);
• veray (turn);
• soot (jump);
• marsh (walk);
• koor (run);
• lii (read);
• dimaandi (ask);
• netwayii (clean);
• laav (wash);
• zhou (play);
• jem (I like); and,
• jem pa (I don’t like).

Distinguish oral and written phonemes in Michif French such as:
• consonants (e.g., R sound; L sound; ZH sound [J or soft G]);
• double vowels/consonants (e.g., oo, ii, aa, nn);
• complex vowels (e.g., oi, aen, enn, ayr); and,
• nasal vowels (e.g., oon, aen, in).

Experiment with the use of present tense verbs and commands (lii moo dakchion), including:
• asi tway (sit down);
• lev tway (stand up);
• veray (turn);
• soot (jump);
• marsh (walk);
• koor (run);
• lii (read);
• dimaandi (ask);
• netwayii (clean);
• laav (wash);
• zhou (play);
• jem (I like); and,
• jem pa (I don’t like).

Repeat simple instructions in familiar contexts through hands on experiences such as:
• beading;
• sewing;
• dancing;
• games;
• baking/cooking; and,
• visual arts.

Interpret simple conversations and respond appropriately to basic interactions, including:
• greetings;
• personal introductions;
• classroom instructions;
• requests for help;
• offering directions within the school and community;
• daily routines and activities; and,
• through visiting.

Identify key information in context, such as:
• days of the week (e.g., laindjii - Monday, mardjii -Tuesday, mikradjii - Wednesday, zhudjii - Thursday, vandradjii - Friday, samdjii Saturday, djiimanch - Sunday);
• past, present, future notions (e.g., eyayr - yesterday, au zhourdjwii - today, dimaen - tomorrow);
• calendar (e.g., lii mwaan - months, lii zannii - years, lii zhour - days);
• time - lii taan - time, lay sayzon - seasons, lii taan - weather; and,
• fill -feelings.

Interact in Michif French while engaging in cultural activities such as:
• cooking;
• sewing;
• cleaning;
• beading;
• playing sports; and,
• dancing.

Explore using Michif French while experiencing land-based activities such as:
• fire making (e.g., with three types of fire: spiritual, cook, warmth);
• story telling (e.g., seasonal, moral, kin, and historical);
• camping, shelter building, hunting;
• fishing, filleting, cooking the fish;
• harvesting (e.g., berry, plant, medicines, and root picking);
• gardening; and,
• canoeing.

Engage in becoming a steward of the land in ways such as:
• caring for waterways;
• examining animals’ habitats and cycles;
• following protocols, assuming responsibilities, and showing respect when using animals/plant life for sustenance; and,
• hunting/trapping traditional ways, contemporary ways, rights, laws, and conservation.

Investigate Michif/Métis kinship systems of la paaraanti such as:
• immediate family;
• extended family;
• adopted family – more formal and more based in history (e.g., Sixties Scoop);
• chosen/found family – less formal and more based in revitalization (e.g., Why was this concept important and a very common informal practice in Michif/Métis communities? What is considered family by Michif/Métis communities? Why do we call some people “aunty” or ma taant even if they aren’t related by blood?); and,
• genealogy/movement of personal families (e.g., consult Red River-based archives of the St. Boniface Historical Society).

Explore the historical and contemporary significance of local areas around Saskatchewan such as:
• Batoche [e.g., battle area, town site, river lots, the East village, Caron house, Duck Lake and Tourond’s Coulee (Fish Creek), Hivernant site (Petite ville)];
• Back to Batoche celebration grounds;
• South Branch House Trading Post; and,
• St. Laurent ferry, Our Lady of Lourdes Shrine, and the buffalo pit.

Plan ways to contribute to Michif French language revitalization, such as:
• sharing the language with other classes and the school community;
• sharing the language with other schools and communities;
• creating ways to increase visibility and accessibility of the language (e.g., labels, community signs, posters, social media); and,
• assisting in creating resources for future generations.

Prepare to interpret written texts and oral communications, before listening or viewing, using strategies such as:
• making predictions;
• using prior knowledge;
• using personal experience; and,
• visual aids.

Interpret written texts and oral communications, during listening and reading, using strategies such as:
• using supplementary information (e.g., summary, illustrations, translations);
• recognizing physical (e.g., gestures and facial expressions) and auditory (e.g., laughter, volume and tone) cues;
• examining context clues and key words to make connections between new information, prior knowledge and personal experience;
• discussing different spelling and pronunciation from another language (e.g., lii frambwoiz [Michif French] – les framboises [French] – raspberries [English]; shiikawk [Michif French] –sikâk [Plains Cree] – skunk [English]) to decipher words; and,
• applying strategies to help cope with information containing unknown elements (e.g., ask for clarification or repetition when needed, indicate lack of understanding).

Recognize the use of present and past tense verbs, including;
• bataazh (harvest);
• shass (hunt);
• pyayzh (trap);
• batchiyiir (build);
• fayr la paysh (fishing);
• condjwiir (row);
• promnii (visit);
• maanzh (eat);
• paarl (speak);
• daans (dance);
• shaant (sing);
• zhoue (play);
• protijay(protect);
• kwiir (cook); and,
• kood (sew).

Apply oral and written phonemes in Michif French such as:
• consonants (e.g., R sound; L sound; ZH sound [J or soft G]);
• double vowels (e.g., oo, ii, aa);
• complex vowels (e.g., oi, aen, enn); and,
• nasal vowels (oon, aen, in).

Interpret the use of present and past tense verbs, including:
• bataazh (harvest);
• shaas (hunt);
• fayr la paysh (fishing);
• batchiyiir (build?);
• la paysh (trap);
• condjwiir (row);
• promnii (visit);
• maanzh (eat);
• paarl (speak);
• daans (dance);
• shaant (sing);
• zhoue (play);
• protijay (protect?);
• kwiir (cook); and,
• kood (sew).

Follow basic imperative oral instructions through hands-on experiences such as:
• fire making (e.g., with three types of fire: spiritual, cook, warmth);
• story-telling (e.g., seasonal, moral, kin, and historical);
• camping, shelter building, hunting;
• fishing, filleting, cooking the fish;
• harvesting (e.g., berry, plant, medicines, and root picking);
• gardening;
• canoeing;
• visiting; and,
• taking care of the young and the old.

Pose questions for clarification within contexts such as:
• fire making (e.g., with three types of fire: spiritual, cook, warmth);
• story-telling (e.g., seasonal, moral, kin, and historical);
• camping, shelter building, hunting;
• fishing, filleting, cooking the fish;
• harvesting (berry, plant, medicines, and root picking);
• gardening;
• canoeing;
• visiting; and,
• taking care of the young and the old.

Use common phrases during a phone call (e.g., to family and friends) such as:
• Comma sa va? (How are you?);
• Voustay? (Where are you?);
• Keustchu fay? (What are you doing?);
• Je awt twayr bayn vitt! (Look forward to seeing you soon!);
• Oorevwar (Good-bye!); and,
• Je tadoor/J’temm (I love you.)

Incorporate the following in oral productions:
• evidence of planning (e.g., project strategy, and timelines);
• clear purpose;
• consideration of audience;
• organization (e.g., opening sentence, order of ideas, and word order);
• appropriate tone; and,
• context-specific vocabulary.

Incorporate the following in written productions:
• evidence of planning (e.g., project strategy and timelines, editing drafts);
• clear purpose;
• consideration of audience;
• organization (e.g., opening sentence, order of ideas and word order);
• appropriate tone; and,
• context-specific vocabulary.

Investigate historical and contemporary Michif/Métis involvement with economy, such as:
• traditional ways of trade and current monetary trade;
• dwellings;
• businesses;
• education;
• local resources (e.g., land ownership, waterways, animals);
• local infrastructure;
• transportation; and,
• availability of funding, support and resources for citizens of Métis Nation – Saskatchewan (e.g., post-secondary funding, small business grants).

Explore the historical and contemporary development (e.g., purpose, aims, goals and design) of Michif/Métis governance, such as:
• Red River governance formation;
• Batoche Resistance governance continuation;
• Government of Canada’s response to Métis governance in these histories;
• the Michif Matriarchs and their influence on governance;
• Métis Locals;
• Métis Nation – Saskatchewan.

Prepare to interpret written texts and oral communications, before listening or viewing, using strategies such as:
• making predictions;
• using prior knowledge;
• using personal experience; and,
• visual aids.

Interpret written texts and oral communications, during reading or listening, using strategies such as:
• analyzing supplementary information (e.g., summary, illustrations, translations);
• examining physical (e.g., gestures and facial expressions) and auditory (e.g., laughter, volume and tone) cues;
• use context clues and key words to make connections between new information, prior knowledge and personal experiences;
• applying different spelling and pronunciation from another language (e.g., house - maison [French]- mazoon [Michif French]) to decipher words; and,
• integrating strategies to help cope with information containing unknown elements (e.g., ask for clarification or repetition when needed, indicate lack of understanding).

Interpret the use of present and past tense verbs including:
• vullay (flying);
• port (carry);
• resti (stay);
• vi (live);
• apprand (learn);
• travaii (work);
• guard (protect);
• priserv (preserve);
• rizistii (resist);
• aycrii (write);
• aankoorajii (encourage);
• dikoorajii (discourage);
• bat (fight); and,
• rawcounte (tell).

Use oral and written phonemes in Michif French such as:
• consonants (e.g., R sound; L sound; ZH sound [J or soft G]);
• double vowels (e.g., oo, ii, aa);
• complex vowels (e.g., oi, aen, enn); and,
• nasal vowels (e.g., oon, aen, in).

Interpret the use of present and past tense verbs, including;
• vullay (flying);
• port (carry);
• resti (stay);
• vi (live);
• apprand (learn);
• travaii (work);
• guard (protect);
• priserv (preserve);
• risistii (resist);
• aycrii (write);
• aankoorajii (encourage);
• dikoorajii (discourage);
• bat (fight); and,
• rawcounte (tell).

Participate in conversations by posing and responding to questions on a variety of topics such as:
• local infrastructure (stores, schools, amenities, past and present);
• transportation (past and present);
• personal strengthens and areas for growth with language learning;
• personal future aspirations; and,
• local, regional, provincial, and national levels of Métis/Michif governance.

Incorporate the following in oral productions:
• evidence of planning (e.g., project strategy and timelines);
• clear purpose;
• consideration of audience;
• organization (e.g., introduction, development of ideas, conclusion, flow of ideas);
• appropriate tone; and,
• rich context-specific vocabulary.

Incorporate the following in written productions:
• evidence of planning (e.g., narrative writing organizers, bullet journals);
• clear purpose;
• consideration of audience;
• organization (e.g., introduction, development of ideas, conclusion, flow of ideas);
• appropriate tone;
• rich context-specific vocabulary; and,
• language suitable to text type.
